# Forbidden patterns for shell scripts (*.sh, *.bash). See forbidden-patterns/README.md.
# scaffold-extensions: sh bash
# scaffold-filenames: Dockerfile Dockerfile.* Makefile *.mk
# The second header claims files with no .sh extension: the same RUN/recipe
# lines (curl|bash, rm -rf /, chmod 777) live in Dockerfiles and Makefiles,
# where an extension match never looks.
# Copy to your project as `.forbidden-patterns/shell.txt`.

(^|[^A-Za-z_])(curl|wget)([[:space:]]+[^|]*)?\|[[:space:]]*(sudo[[:space:]]+)?(bash|sh|zsh)([[:space:]]|$)	Piping remote download to a shell — review the script and run it as a separate step
(^|[^A-Za-z_])rm[[:space:]]+((-[a-zA-Z]+|--[a-z]+)[[:space:]]+)+["']?(/|/\*|~|~/\*|\$\{?HOME\}?(/\*)?)["']?([[:space:]]|[;&]|$)	`rm -rf /` (or /*, ~, $HOME) detected — refuse to ship; scope to a specific path
(^|[^A-Za-z_])chmod[[:space:]]+(-[a-zA-Z]+[[:space:]]+)*0?777([[:space:]]|$)	chmod 0?777 — overly permissive; scope to specific user/group bits
(^|[^A-Za-z_])git[[:space:]][^|;&]*[[:space:]]--no-verify([[:space:]]|[;&]|$)	git --no-verify bypasses the pre-commit/commit-msg hooks — fix the failure, don't skip the gate (mark a genuine uninstall with scaffold-allow)
(^|[^A-Za-z_])curl[[:space:]]([^|;&]*[[:space:]])?(-[a-zA-Z]*k[a-zA-Z]*|--insecure)([[:space:]]|$)	curl -k/--insecure disables TLS cert validation (MITM risk) — fix the cert/CA instead
(^|[^A-Za-z_])wget[[:space:]]([^|;&]*[[:space:]])?--no-check-certificate([[:space:]]|$)	wget --no-check-certificate disables TLS cert validation (MITM risk) — fix the cert/CA instead

# Short-form hook bypass. `-n` is git's documented short option for --no-verify on
# `git commit` (git-commit(1)), so the long-form rule above misses a one-character
# evasion of the only machine check between an agent and every pre-commit gate.
# Scoped to `git commit` and to a segment carrying no quote: `git push -n` is
# --dry-run (harmless, and not matched), and `git commit -m "add -n flag"` must
# not fire. Clustered forms (-nm, -an) are covered; `grep -n`, `sort -n`,
# `echo -n`, `ssh -n`, `head -n 5` and `git log -n 5` are not.
(^|[^A-Za-z_])git[[:space:]]+commit[[:space:]]([^|;&"']*[[:space:]])?-[a-zA-Z]*n[a-zA-Z]*([[:space:]]|[;&]|$)	git commit -n is --no-verify in short form — fix the failing check, don't skip the gate (mark a genuine uninstall with scaffold-allow)

# --no-verify skips the hook for ONE commit; core.hooksPath disarms every hook in
# the clone permanently, with no output and no committed artifact to review.
# Deliberately matches ONLY the two values that can never be a real hooks
# directory — `/dev/null` and the empty string — i.e. `git -c
# core.hooksPath=/dev/null commit`, the documented one-liner bypass.
# NOT matched, because each has a legitimate everyday use that no regex can tell
# apart from a bypass: `git config --unset core.hooksPath` (what an uninstaller
# does — this scaffold's own uninstall.sh line 213), a re-point to another
# directory (`.husky`, `githooks`, `hooks` are all real conventions; this repo
# ships a `githooks/` dir), and `git -c core.hooksPath=<tmpdir>` (what a hook test
# harness does — tests/cases/14,15,18 here). An earlier draft of this rule matched
# all three and put four errors into this repo's own self-lint run.
(^|[^A-Za-z_])git[[:space:]][^|;&]*core\.hooksPath[[:space:]=]+(["']?/dev/null|""|'')	core.hooksPath=/dev/null (or empty) disarms every hook in this clone — fix the failing check instead (mark a genuine uninstall with scaffold-allow)

# Git discipline (AGENTS.md > Git discipline). agent-precheck feeds this file to
# Claude PreToolUse / Cursor beforeShellExecution, which is the only point at
# which a destructive git command can still be stopped — after it runs there is
# nothing left to gate. Each rule is anchored to its subcommand: a bare `-f` arm
# would also block `git add -f`, `git config -f`, `git branch -f` and
# `git checkout -f`, which are benign.
(^|[^A-Za-z_])git[[:space:]]+push[[:space:]]([^|;&]*[[:space:]])?(--force(-with-lease)?([=[:space:]]|[;&]|$)|-f([[:space:]]|[;&]|$))	git push --force/-f/--force-with-lease rewrites published history — needs explicit instruction for this push, and on main/master ask first even then
(^|[^A-Za-z_])git[[:space:]]+commit[[:space:]]([^|;&"']*[[:space:]])?--amend([[:space:]]|[;&]|$)	git commit --amend rewrites history — never on a pushed commit; scaffold-allow a vetted local amend
(^|[^A-Za-z_])git[[:space:]]+checkout[[:space:]]+(--[[:space:]]+)?\.([[:space:]]|[;&]|$)	git checkout . discards every uncommitted change in the tree — confirm, and name the specific paths instead

# Optional: the two tree-destroying commands (AGENTS.md > Git discipline names
# `reset --hard` and `clean -fd` alongside `checkout .`). OFF by default, because
# unlike `checkout .` both are also the standard body of a FIXTURE-RESET helper —
# a test harness or CI script that hard-resets and cleans a throwaway clone
# between cases. This scaffold's own tests/lib/common.sh:17-18 is exactly that,
# and a line-oriented grep cannot tell a scratch clone from the developer's
# working tree: the target repo is chosen by a `cd` on some earlier line.
# `git clean` is scoped to the FORCING forms only (`-f`/`--force`): `-d`/`-x`
# alone are inert (git refuses to delete without `-f` or `-i`), so an earlier
# `[dfx]` draft fired on `git clean -nd` — the exact dry-run this rule's own
# message recommends — and on `--dry-run -d`.
# Enable both in an application repo with no fixture-reset scripts; if you have
# one, enable them and mark that helper `scaffold-allow`.
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])git[[:space:]]+reset[[:space:]]([^|;&]*[[:space:]])?--hard([[:space:]]|[;&]|$)	git reset --hard destroys uncommitted work silently — confirm first and tag a restore point (claude-code#34327 lost 12 unpushed commits this way)
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])git[[:space:]]+clean[[:space:]]([^|;&]*[[:space:]])?(-[a-zA-Z]*f[a-zA-Z]*|--force)([[:space:]]|[;&]|$)	git clean -f/-fd deletes untracked files irreversibly — dry-run it with `git clean -nd` and confirm the exact paths first

# "Never print, cat, or echo secret files" (operational-rules.md). `cat .env`
# mid-debug is the named agent habit: the values then live in the transcript, the
# CI log and any pasted diff forever, and every one of them needs rotating.
# Claude's `Read(**/.env)` deny does not cover the Bash route; this rule does.
#
# Scoping, all of it learned from false positives:
#  * Only READERS. `echo`/`printf` were dropped from the command list: they print
#    their ARGUMENT, never a file, so they can never leak a .env — while
#    `echo .env >> .gitignore` and `printf %s .env` are stock setup-script lines.
#    (`echo $(cat .env)` is still caught, by the `cat`.)
#  * Arguments between the command and the path must look like an option, a
#    number, or a path (contain `.` or `/`). That keeps `head -n 20 .env` and
#    `cat cert.pem privkey.pem` matching while prose such as `# tail the .env`
#    or `# read the tail of .env` cannot reach the path.
#  * The rest of the line must contain no `>`. A redirect after the path is a
#    COPY, not a print (`cat .env > /tmp/backup`, `cat "$SRC/.env" > "$DST/.env"`),
#    and a redirect before it is a WRITE (`echo FOO=bar >.env`, `cat >id_rsa`).
#  * The path must END at `.env` (or `.env.local`, `.env.prod`, …), so
#    `.env.example`, `.env.template`, `.env.sample` and `id_rsa.pub` never match.
#  * `.pem` alone is NOT a secret marker — `fullchain.pem`/`server.pem` are public
#    certificates and bundling them is routine deploy work. Only a .pem whose
#    name carries `key`/`priv`/`secret` matches; `.p12`/`.pfx`/`id_rsa`/
#    `~/.aws/credentials`/`~/.netrc` are key material by definition.
(^|[^A-Za-z_])(cat|less|more|head|tail|strings|xxd|od|base64)([[:space:]]+(-[^[:space:]|;&>"']+|[0-9]+|[^[:space:]|;&>"']*[./][^[:space:]|;&>"']*))*[[:space:]]+["']?[^[:space:]"'>|;&]*\.env(\.(local|prod|production|staging|dev|development))?(([[:space:]]|[;&|)"'])[^>]*)?$	Never print a .env — the values land in the transcript and CI log forever and all need rotating; check a length or a hash instead
(^|[^A-Za-z_])(cat|less|more|head|tail|strings|xxd|od|base64)([[:space:]]+(-[^[:space:]|;&>"']+|[0-9]+|[^[:space:]|;&>"']*[./][^[:space:]|;&>"']*))*[[:space:]]+["']?[^[:space:]"'>|;&]*(id_(rsa|dsa|ecdsa|ed25519)|\.(p12|pfx)|\.aws/credentials|\.netrc|(key|priv|secret)[^[:space:]"'>|;&]*\.pem)(([[:space:]]|[;&|)"'])[^>]*)?$	Never print a key/credential file (privkey.pem, id_rsa, .p12, ~/.aws/credentials, ~/.netrc) — verify it by length or fingerprint, never by dumping it
